---
description: Every delete / destructive action in the dashboard or frontend must ask for confirmation in a modal
alwaysApply: true
---

# Confirm before delete (always)

Whenever you add or edit **any delete, remove, clear, purge, or other irreversible action** (single row, bulk selection, drawer button, dropdown item, image removal, history clear, notification delete, etc.), the user **must confirm in a popup modal first**.

## Dashboard (`dashboard/`)

- Use the shared promise-based hook — never `window.confirm` / `alert`:

```tsx
import { useConfirm } from "@/components/providers/ConfirmProvider";

const confirm = useConfirm();

async function onDelete() {
  const ok = await confirm({
    title: `Delete “${item.name}”?`,
    description: "What gets removed and whether it can be undone.",
    confirmLabel: "Delete item",
  });
  if (!ok) return;
  // …call the API
}
```

- `ConfirmProvider` is mounted in `dashboard/src/main.tsx`; `tone` defaults to `"destructive"` (red button + trash icon). Use `tone: "warning"` for non-delete risky actions.
- Title names the exact thing (e.g. `Delete order #NIY-…?`); description says what is removed and that it **cannot be undone** when that's true.
- Bulk deletes: say how many items, and warn about items the API will skip.
- Show a loading state on the trigger while the request runs and report success / error afterwards.

## Frontend (`frontend/`)

- Same rule: use a shadcn `Dialog` / alert-dialog style modal for deletes (cart item removal may stay instant only if an **Undo** is offered).

## API side

- Deletes stay behind elevated roles and server-side guards (e.g. don't hard-delete paid orders); the modal is UX, not security.
- Document new delete endpoints in `backend-api/API.md`.
